# Line endings.
#
# Shell scripts run inside Linux containers, or under Git Bash on Windows.
# Both break on CRLF — a container entrypoint with \r fails to exec at all,
# and bash reports `$'\r': command not found`. A Windows customer cloning
# with core.autocrlf=true would otherwise get exactly that on first run, so
# these files are pinned to LF regardless of anyone's git settings.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows scripts are the other way round. cmd.exe mis-parses a batch
# file with LF-only line endings.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Let git decide for everything else.
*               text=auto

# Development-only files, kept out of the ZIP a professor receives.
.github/            export-ignore
frontend/           export-ignore
# The dev launcher needs a venv and the source tree, neither of which a
# professor has - and a second "Start" file in that folder is a question
# nobody should have to ask. Wildcards because git attribute patterns
# cannot contain a space.
*Dev.bat            export-ignore
scripts/dev.ps1     export-ignore
